<JournalEntry
  title="Music Discovery Engines"
  date="September 1, 2022"
  prose={false}
>
    <p class="text-lg">
      lately, music discovery in the streaming era has felt really broken to me.
      want to dissect why i feel this way, so dumping a bunch of thoughts about
      this + other music discovery methods here:
    </p>
    <br />
    <ul class="custom-bullets pl-5 space-y-1 text-lg">
      <li>
        playlists are spotify's key differentiator vs. other streaming
        platforms, in an industry where the main offerings (music libraries) are
        now commoditized. unfortunately, this seems to be the main mode through
        which people discover new music today.
      </li>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-lg">
        <li>
          the power dynamic of the platform with independent artists feels icky
        </li>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-lg">
          <li>
            making it onto spotify playlists (which are now mostly
            "algorithmically" generated) is make-or-break for artists these days
          </li>
        </ul>
      </ul>
      <li>
        more music is available to consumers than ever before, yet music
        discovery feels super centralized in these playlists in a culturally
        decrepit way
      </li>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-lg">
        <li>
          for something as personal as the music you listen to, it feels
          soulless to outsource the joy of music selection to bots for the sake
          of convenience
        </li>
        <li>
          spotify pushes songs that share features with tracks you already
          listen to, optimizing for recognition / comfort. you're much less
          likely to encounter that earth-shattering experience of being blown
          away by something totally new that really hits for you
        </li>
        <li>
          in college, i used to say that my dream job was <a
            href="https://www.buzzfeed.com/reggieugwu/the-unsung-heroes-of-the-music-streaming-boom"
            >spotify playlist curator</a
          >. i've now 180'd on this / i'd guess that this job probably doesn't
          exist anymore given how much they've leaned into ML-driven
          recommendations
        </li>
      </ul>

      <li>
        with each new technology, the industry shifts to adapt and it's worth
        thinking about incentives for the platform & artist and how that changes
        the way music is made:
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-lg">
          <li>
            practices like <a
              href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Controversy_over_fake_artists_on_Spotify"
              >fake artists</a
            > are incentivized: spotify essentially pays studios to churn out "stock
            music" and lists these songs under fake names, in order to reproduce
            the sounds of their popular ambient music playlists at a discount (undercutting
            the earnings of real artists)
          </li>
          <li>
            there's talk of spotify introducing programs where artists trade a
            portion of their song's royalties for priority status on an
            algorithmically generated playlist... feels dystopian af
            (thematically similar to <a
              href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Payola">payola</a
            >)
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
        imo the most underrated way to discover new artists is to look up which
        producer was responsible for a song you love, then listen to the other
        artists they've produced for
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-lg">
          <li>
            the production of a record molds the essence of a song in ways that
            can be very hard to pinpoint or describe, but we emotionally latch
            onto it all the same: underlying groove/beat, tonal
            balance/frequency weighting, synths/sounds/samples used, etc.
          </li>

          <li>
            i like to think of songs from the same producer as having the same
            "flavor of creativity" even when they sound quite different
          </li>

          <li>
            this is a fun way to unravel the social networks that exist within
            your favorite pockets of the music industry
          </li>

          <li>
            this discovery method doesn't quite work with smaller indie artists,
            who tend to self-produce until they gain more traction
          </li>

          <li>
            maybe this resonates more with me because i like the idea of
            appreciating the work of those behind the scenes who are responsible
            for a project (like the writers behind a comedy act, the crew behind
            a film set, or the engineers behind the tech)
          </li>

          <li>
            producers i've been digging recently:
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-lg">
              <li>
                <a
                  href="https://www.discogs.com/artist/766594-Gus-Seyffert?superFilter=Production"
                  >Gus Seyffert</a
                > (associated artists: <a
                  href="https://youtu.be/7teJk4RyW9Q?si=EcdGHaVlyf3sK2yv"
                  >Bedouine</a
                >, <a href="https://youtu.be/kJ4s3G7hgR4?si=6sp867m9O3kYz2l1"
                  >Michael Kiwanuka</a
                >, <a href="https://youtu.be/GYcOSA4K2s8?si=B9KaiGZIaP2mRUNm"
                  >Michelle Branch</a
                >)
              </li>
              <li>
                <a
                  href="https://www.discogs.com/artist/2587679-Adam-Jaffrey?superFilter=Production"
                  >Adam Jaffrey</a
                > (associated artists: <a
                  href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ImllpvDwbQ8"
                  >Tom Misch</a
                >, <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=f6noiVByjzM"
                  >Palace</a
                >, <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xnx0Iim3Vo8"
                  >Kamaal Williams</a
                >)
              </li>
              <li>
                <a
                  href="https://www.discogs.com/artist/1342531-Patrick-Wimberly?superFilter=Production"
                  >Patrick Wimberly</a
                > (associated artists: <a
                  href="https://youtu.be/d7PPjEB2QZQ?si=k4lIiwy4ouK6anru"
                  >Beyonce</a
                >, <a href="https://youtu.be/tmozGmGoJuw?si=2UrgA3XH4cvOrjgE"
                  >MGMT</a
                >, <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3b0HXEymmSI"
                  >Anna of the North</a
                >)
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>

          <li>
            producers might not be so underrated anymore! happy to see more of
            them becoming household names (e.g., Mark Ronson, Jack Antonoff) and
            it's nice they're getting shouted out more by the stars they work
            with
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
        discovering music through friends has been around since the beginning of
        time but imo is somehow still underrated
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-lg">
          <li>
            people don't stalk the spotify "friend activity" sidebar enough,
            myself included these days - i used to text friends about what they
            were listening to all the time in college and it led to so much
            sharing!
          </li>

          <li>
            live music is key. will go to virtually any concert that friends
            suggest, whether or not i know the artist
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-lg">
              <li>
                exception is artists so popular that i will hear the people
                around me singing poorly more than i will be able to hear the
                artists themselves
              </li>

              <li>
                need to go to more shows in sf, in boston i averaged 1.5 per
                month apparently
                <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-lg">
                  <li>
                    used to pick venues i liked and showed up randomly without
                    knowing the artist, need to bring that back
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </li>
            </ul>
            <li>
              strangers / new friends: met someone at a party a while back who
              runs a music substack with monthly obscure album recommendations
              and i've quite enjoyed most of them
            </li>
          </li>
          <li>
            friends gifted me vinyl records for years before i finally got a
            record player to listen to them. i love:
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-lg">
              <li>
                sitting down with an album i haven't heard that made a friend
                think of me
              </li>

              <li>learning more about the record from the liner notes</li>

              <li>listening to tracks in the intended order</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
        maybe overrated, but some of my most cherished music discovery memories
        have involved vinyl records
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-lg">
          <li>
            bars inspired by japanese <a
              href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jazz_kissa">listening cafes</a
            > are unsurprisingly insanely good at curating their music collection
            and have introduced me to phenomenal hidden gems
          </li>

          <li>
            crate digging in the used records section of record shops lets me
            examine the most worn out records of strangers' collections, in
            essence gracing me with a glimpse into their personal histories
          </li>

          <li>
            probably the main reason these topics have been on my mind in the
            first place: last week i reserved 2 hrs in the minneapolis central
            library's "vinyl revival listening room." it was a small, unassuming
            space with a stereo turntable and headphones connected to it. you
            can bring your own record, or choose from a rotating selection of
            albums curated by local musicians, DJs, and library staff. what a
            delightful experience... if i lived here i'd come to this place all
            the time for the curated picks, whether or not i owned my own
            turntable already
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
        some of the strongest responses i've had to music are from songs i
        discovered through tv shows
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-lg">
          <li>
            often instant emotional ties to memorable associated scenes from the
            show
          </li>

          <li>tv shows often try to break newer, unheard of artists</li>

          <li>
            a ridiculous fraction of my top tracks last year were from the
            Insecure HBO soundtrack
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-lg">
              <li>they somehow scored Solange as a music consultant??</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
        i enjoy music review publications. there's something nostalgic about
        them, if only because people don't seem to pay much attention to them
        anymore
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-lg">
          <li>
            everyday language is typically pretty lacking in its vocabulary for
            describing music, so it's fascinating to observe how these
            journalists effusively write about elements of the records they love
          </li>

          <li>
            sometimes using these sites (rateyourmusic, rolling stones,
            pitchfork) feels like being prescribed what to listen to, which i
            don't always love, so i treat it more like browsing "music news" to
            be aware of what's out there
          </li>

          <li>
            not quite the same thing but i'm pretty proud of being nearly a day
            1 fan of the Song Exploder podcast. loved diving into the
            discography of numerous artists after hearing them talk about their
            music creation processes on the pod
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
    </ul>
</JournalEntry>
